const concepts = [
  {id:'sec-10-1',heading:'10.1–10.2 · Heat, temperature and equilibrium',intro:'Temperature describes thermal state; heat describes energy in transit.',points:[
    'Temperature is an objective measure of hotness. Touch is subjective and limited; a thermometer uses a measurable thermometric property that varies with temperature.',
    'Heat is energy transferred between systems solely because of a temperature difference. It flows spontaneously from higher to lower temperature; its SI unit is joule (J). Temperature is measured in kelvin (K).',
    'When system and surroundings reach the same temperature, net heat transfer ceases. Heat is not a substance stored inside a body; internal energy is a state property.'
  ],revision:['Temperature measures thermal state; heat is energy crossing a boundary because temperatures differ.','Heat flows hot → cold until thermal equilibrium; SI units are J and K.','A thermometer reads a thermometric property; touch alone is unreliable.']},
  {id:'sec-10-2',heading:'10.3 · Measuring temperature and temperature scales',intro:'A scale assigns values using reproducible fixed points and equal intervals.',points:[
    'Liquid-in-glass thermometers use volume expansion; gas thermometers use pressure at fixed volume. Different thermometric properties may disagree between calibration points.',
    'At standard pressure, ice and steam points are 0 °C and 100 °C, or 32 °F and 212 °F. Linear interpolation gives (t<sub>F</sub>−32)/180 = t<sub>C</sub>/100.',
    'Thus t<sub>F</sub>=(9/5)t<sub>C</sub>+32 and t<sub>C</sub>=(5/9)(t<sub>F</sub>−32). Celsius and Fahrenheit differ in both zero and interval size.'
  ],revision:['Use tF=(9/5)tC+32 and tC=(5/9)(tF−32).','A scale needs a thermometric property plus fixed reference points.','The Celsius and Fahrenheit ice/steam points are (0,100) °C and (32,212) °F.'],figure:svgScale},
  {id:'sec-10-3',heading:'10.4 · Ideal-gas equation and absolute temperature',intro:'Low-density gases provide a common route to an absolute scale.',points:[
    'For a fixed amount of gas, Boyle’s law gives PV=constant at fixed T; Charles’ law gives V/T=constant at fixed P. Combining them gives PV/T=constant.',
    'For μ moles, PV=μRT, where R=8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹. At constant volume P∝T; gas-thermometer lines extrapolate toward zero pressure at absolute zero.',
    'T=t<sub>C</sub>+273.15 K. Kelvin and Celsius have equal interval sizes, but different origins. Real gases depart from ideal behaviour, especially at low temperature; use dilute gas and extrapolation.'
  ],revision:['PV=μRT; Boyle: PV const at fixed T; Charles: V/T const at fixed P.','Kelvin temperature is T=tC+273.15; never put Celsius into the ideal-gas law.','Absolute zero is inferred by dilute-gas extrapolation; real gases do not remain ideal there.']},
  {id:'sec-10-4',heading:'10.5 · Linear, area and volume expansion',intro:'For small temperature intervals, expansion is proportional to original size and ΔT.',points:[
    'Linear expansivity α is defined by ΔL/L=αΔT, so L=L₀(1+αΔT). Area expansivity β=ΔA/(AΔT); for an isotropic solid β≈2α.',
    'Proof: ΔA=aΔb+bΔa+ΔaΔb; put Δa=αaΔT and Δb=αbΔT. The product term is second order and negligible, leaving ΔA/A≈2αΔT.',
    'Volume expansivity γ=ΔV/(VΔT). For a cube, ΔV=(L+ΔL)³−L³≈3L²ΔL, hence γ≈3α. These relations assume small expansion and isotropic solids.'
  ],revision:['ΔL/L=αΔT; ΔA/A≈2αΔT; ΔV/V≈3αΔT for small isotropic solid expansion.','Area proof drops the second-order ΔaΔb term; cube proof drops (ΔL)² and (ΔL)³.','Always state the initial dimension and temperature interval used.'],figure:svgExpansion,examples:[
    {badge:'NCERT Example 10.1 · Proof',title:'Area expansivity of a rectangular sheet',question:'Show that the area-expansion coefficient of a solid sheet is twice its linear expansivity.',steps:['Let its sides be a and b. A rise ΔT gives Δa=αaΔT and Δb=αbΔT.','The area change is ΔA=aΔb+bΔa+ΔaΔb=αabΔT(2+αΔT).','Since αΔT≪1, neglect the second-order term: ΔA/A≈2αΔT.'],answer:'β=2α for small, isotropic expansion.'},
    {badge:'NCERT Example 10.2 · Solved',title:'Heating an iron ring to fit a wheel',question:'At 27 °C, an iron ring is 5.231 m across and a wheel rim is 5.243 m. Find the ring temperature required; use α=1.20×10⁻⁵ K⁻¹.',steps:['Use D₂=D₁[1+α(T₂−T₁)] because every length of the free ring expands by the same fractional amount.','T₂=27+[5.243/5.231−1]/(1.20×10⁻⁵)=218.4 °C.','A small expansion is enough because the diameter deficit is only 0.012 m.'],answer:'About 218 °C, as in the NCERT rounded result.'}
  ]},
  {id:'sec-10-5',heading:'10.5 · Liquids, gases and constrained expansion',intro:'Expansion depends on state, temperature and mechanical constraints.',points:[
    'Liquids have volume expansivity γ. A liquid in a vessel appears to expand by γ<sub>app</sub>=γ<sub>liquid</sub>−γ<sub>vessel</sub>; the vessel expands too.',
    'Water is anomalous between 0 °C and 4 °C: it contracts when warmed, has maximum density at 4 °C, and expands on cooling below 4 °C. Lakes freeze at the surface, helping aquatic life survive below.',
    'For an ideal gas at constant pressure, V∝T, so γ=1/T. If a rod is fully constrained, total strain is zero; mechanical strain cancels αΔT and |σ|=Yα|ΔT|. Heating gives compression and cooling gives tension; expansion joints prevent damaging stress.'
  ],revision:['Liquid apparent expansion: γapp=γliquid−γcontainer.','Water has maximum density at 4 °C; below 4 °C it expands as it cools.','At fixed pressure ideal-gas γ=1/T; fully restrained rod stress magnitude is Yα|ΔT|.'],examples:[
    {badge:'Worked check · Thermal stress',title:'Fully restrained thermal strain',question:'A steel rail cannot expand. Find the compressive thermal stress for α=1.2×10⁻⁵ K⁻¹, Y=2×10¹¹ Pa and a 10 K rise.',steps:['The free thermal strain would be αΔT=1.2×10⁻⁴.','Rigid supports impose an equal-and-opposite elastic strain.','The stress magnitude is YαΔT=(2×10¹¹)(1.2×10⁻⁴).'],answer:'2.4×10⁷ Pa compression. (This is the chapter’s rail illustration, solved explicitly.)'}
  ]},
  {id:'sec-10-6',heading:'10.6 · Heat capacity and specific heat',intro:'Temperature change depends on mass, material and the amount of heat transferred.',points:[
    'Heat capacity S=ΔQ/ΔT is for the whole body (J K⁻¹). Specific heat s=ΔQ/(mΔT) is per unit mass (J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹); hence ΔQ=msΔT when no phase change occurs.',
    'Molar heat capacity C=ΔQ/(μΔT), in J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹. For a gas, specify constant pressure C<sub>p</sub> or constant volume C<sub>v</sub>; generally C<sub>p</sub>&gt;C<sub>v</sub>.',
    'Specific heats depend on material and temperature. Water’s large s≈4186 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹ makes it useful as a coolant and moderator of day–night and land–sea temperature changes.'
  ],revision:['S=ΔQ/ΔT; s=ΔQ/(mΔT); C=ΔQ/(μΔT).','Use Q=msΔT only while the substance remains in one phase.','For gases name the condition: Cp or Cv; water has unusually high specific heat.'],examples:[
    {badge:'NCERT Example 10.3 · Solved',title:'Specific heat of aluminium by calorimetry',question:'A 0.047 kg aluminium sphere at 100 °C is placed in 0.25 kg water and a 0.14 kg copper calorimeter, both at 20 °C. Final temperature: 23 °C. Find sAl.',steps:['Heat lost by Al = 0.047sAl(100−23).','Heat gained = [0.25(4180)+0.14(386)](23−20)=3,297 J.','Set lost equal to gained: sAl=3297/[0.047×77]=911 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.'],answer:'sAl≈0.911 kJ kg⁻¹ K⁻¹, consistent with the NCERT worked result.'},
    {badge:'NCERT Example 10.4 · Solved',title:'Finding latent heat of fusion',question:'Mix 0.15 kg ice at 0 °C with 0.30 kg water at 50 °C; final temperature is 6.7 °C. Find Lf; sw=4186 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.',steps:['Hot-water heat loss: 0.30×4186×(50−6.7)=54,376 J.','Ice first melts, then the melt warms: 0.15Lf+0.15×4186×6.7.','Equate gain and loss: Lf=(54,376−4,207)/0.15.'],answer:'Lf≈3.34×10⁵ J kg⁻¹.'}
  ]},
  {id:'sec-10-7',heading:'10.7 · Calorimetry: heat balance',intro:'An isolated mixture conserves energy: heat lost equals heat gained.',points:[
    'A calorimeter limits heat exchange with surroundings. In the ideal isolated model, ΣQ=0; hot components lose energy and colder components gain it until one common final temperature is reached.',
    'For sensible heating/cooling use Q=ms(T<sub>f</sub>−T<sub>i</sub>) with sign. Include the calorimeter’s own heat capacity or water equivalent.',
    'For a phase change include latent heat too. Check that the predicted final state is physically possible before solving; account for environmental losses if the apparatus is not well insulated.'
  ],revision:['Isolated system: heat lost + heat gained = 0.','Include water, calorimeter and every object; a water equivalent is its heat capacity divided by sw.','Add latent-heat terms at phase transitions and check the assumed final phase.']},
  {id:'sec-10-8',heading:'10.8 · Change of state and latent heat',intro:'During a phase transition, supplied energy changes arrangement rather than temperature.',points:[
    'Fusion is solid→liquid and freezing is liquid→solid. Vaporisation is liquid→gas and condensation is gas→liquid. During an equilibrium transition, both phases coexist and temperature remains constant at fixed pressure.',
    'Latent heat per unit mass is L=Q/m (J kg⁻¹); Q=mL. Use Lf for fusion and Lv for vaporisation. Heating a sample across several stages requires summing sensible-heat and latent-heat intervals.',
    'Evaporation occurs at the surface below boiling and cools the remaining liquid; boiling is bulk vapour formation at the pressure-dependent boiling point. The triple point is where solid, liquid and vapour coexist; a phase diagram shows the coexistence curves.'
  ],revision:['At a phase plateau, Q=mL while T is constant and both phases coexist.','Evaporation is surface-only below boiling; boiling occurs throughout at a pressure-dependent temperature.','Triple point: solid + liquid + vapour coexist. Regelation is pressure melting followed by refreezing.'],figure:svgHeating,examples:[
    {badge:'NCERT Example 10.5 · Solved',title:'Ice at −12 °C to steam at 100 °C',question:'Find the heat to turn 3 kg ice at −12 °C into steam at 100 °C. Use sice=2100, sw=4186 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹, Lf=3.35×10⁵ and Lv=2.256×10⁶ J kg⁻¹.',steps:['Warm ice to 0 °C: Q₁=3×2100×12=75,600 J.','Melt it: Q₂=3×3.35×10⁵=1,005,000 J; warm water to 100 °C: Q₃=3×4186×100=1,255,800 J.','Vaporise at 100 °C: Q₄=3×2.256×10⁶=6,768,000 J. Add all four stages.'],answer:'Q=9.1044×10⁶ J≈9.1 MJ.'}
  ]},
  {id:'sec-10-8-pressure',heading:'10.8 · Pressure, regelation and phase diagrams',intro:'Phase boundaries shift with pressure; water is a notable exception.',points:[
    'Melting and boiling temperatures depend on pressure. Increased pressure lowers ice’s melting point because liquid water is denser than ice; a weighted wire can melt ice beneath it and water refreezes above it (regelation).',
    'A P–T phase diagram separates solid, liquid and vapour regions. Sublimation, fusion and vaporisation curves mark coexistence of two phases; their intersection is the triple point.',
    'Water’s triple point is 273.16 K at about 611 Pa. Its fusion line has negative slope; the fusion line of CO₂ and most substances slopes positively. At ordinary atmospheric pressure CO₂ sublimes instead of forming a liquid.'
  ],revision:['Phase boundaries depend on pressure; ice melts under increased pressure because liquid water is denser.','The three phase-boundary curves meet at the triple point (water: 273.16 K, about 611 Pa).','Solid CO₂ sublimes at 1 atm; water’s fusion curve slopes negatively.'],figure:svgPhase},
  {id:'sec-10-9',heading:'10.9 · Heat transfer: conduction and convection',intro:'Heat can move by neighbouring interactions, bulk flow or electromagnetic radiation.',points:[
    'Conduction transfers energy between neighbouring parts without bulk matter flow. For a uniform insulated bar at steady state, H=K A(T<sub>hot</sub>−T<sub>cold</sub>)/L; K is thermal conductivity (W m⁻¹ K⁻¹).',
    'For layers in series the heat current is common and thermal resistances add: R<sub>th</sub>=L/(KA), H=ΔT/ΣR<sub>th</sub>. Metals conduct well; trapped air and foams insulate.',
    'Convection requires moving fluid. Natural convection uses buoyancy (warm, less-dense fluid rises); forced convection uses a pump or fan. Land/sea breezes reverse from day to night; Earth’s rotation shapes large-scale convection into trade-wind circulation.'
  ],revision:['Conduction: H=KAΔT/L in steady state; no bulk material flow.','Series layers: thermal resistances L/(KA) add.','Convection transports heat with fluid motion; natural flow uses buoyancy, forced flow uses a pump/fan.'],examples:[
    {badge:'NCERT Example 10.6 · Solved',title:'Steel–copper junction temperature',question:'Steel (L=0.15 m, K=50.2) and copper (L=0.10 m, K=385 W m⁻¹ K⁻¹) are joined in series. The steel area is 2A and copper area A; ends are at 300 °C and 0 °C. Find junction temperature.',steps:['At steady state, heat currents are equal: 2(50.2)(300−T)/0.15 = 385T/0.10.','Cancel the common area and solve 669.3(300−T)=3850T.','The same current passes every cross-section; side losses are neglected.'],answer:'T≈44.4 °C, consistent with the NCERT worked result.'},
    {badge:'NCERT Example 10.7 · Solved',title:'Two rods in series: junction and equivalent K',question:'Equal 0.10 m rods of iron (K₁=79) and brass (K₂=109 W m⁻¹ K⁻¹), each area 0.02 m², have ends at 373 K and 273 K. Find junction temperature, equivalent conductivity and heat current.',steps:['Equal steady heat currents give K₁(T₁−T₀)=K₂(T₀−T₂), so T₀=(K₁T₁+K₂T₂)/(K₁+K₂).','T₀=(79×373+109×273)/188=315 K; K′=2K₁K₂/(K₁+K₂)=91.6 W m⁻¹ K⁻¹.','H=K′A(T₁−T₂)/(2L)=91.6×0.02×100/0.20.'],answer:'T₀=315 K; K′=91.6 W m⁻¹ K⁻¹; H≈916 W.'}
  ]},
  {id:'sec-10-9-radiation',heading:'10.9 · Radiation and blackbody laws',intro:'Thermal radiation crosses a vacuum; its spectrum and intensity reveal temperature.',points:[
    'Thermal radiation is electromagnetic energy emitted because of temperature; unlike conduction or convection it needs no material medium. Dark, matte surfaces absorb and emit more effectively than shiny reflective ones.',
    'A blackbody absorbs all incident radiation and is an ideal emitter. Its continuous spectrum is universal at a given temperature. Wien’s displacement law gives λ<sub>max</sub>T=2.9×10⁻³ m K: hotter blackbodies peak at shorter wavelength.',
    'Stefan–Boltzmann law: P=eσAT⁴ (σ=5.67×10⁻⁸ W m⁻² K⁻⁴; e=1 for a blackbody). In surroundings at T<sub>s</sub>, net radiative loss is eσA(T⁴−T<sub>s</sub>⁴). A thermos uses silvered walls and a vacuum gap to reduce radiation and conduction/convection.'
  ],revision:['Radiation is electromagnetic energy and needs no medium; only this mode works across vacuum.','Wien: λmaxT=2.9×10⁻³ m K. Stefan–Boltzmann: P=eσAT⁴; net P=eσA(T⁴−Ts⁴).','Black/dull surfaces are strong absorbers and emitters; shiny surfaces reflect radiation.'],figure:svgSpectrum},
  {id:'sec-10-10',heading:'10.10 · Newton’s law of cooling',intro:'For small excess temperatures, heat-loss rate is proportional to the body’s excess temperature.',points:[
    'For body temperature T and surroundings T<sub>s</sub>, −dQ/dt=k(T−T<sub>s</sub>) for a small temperature difference. k depends on exposed area and surface; multiple transfer modes may contribute.',
    'If the body has mass m and specific heat s, dQ=ms dT. Thus d(T−T<sub>s</sub>)/dt=−K(T−T<sub>s</sub>), where K=k/(ms).',
    'Integrating gives ln(T−T<sub>s</sub>)=−Kt+C, or T=T<sub>s</sub>+(T₀−T<sub>s</sub>)e⁻ᴷᵗ. It is an approximation for small excess temperature; a plot of ln(T−T<sub>s</sub>) against time is straight.'
  ],revision:['Cooling rate ∝ (T−Ts) only for a small excess temperature.','Integrate to T−Ts=(T0−Ts)e⁻ᴷᵗ; the ln(excess)–time slope is −K.','Compare mean excess temperatures for textbook finite-interval estimates.'],figure:svgCooling,examples:[
    {badge:'NCERT Example 10.8 · Solved',title:'Cooling time from Newton’s law',question:'A pan cools 94→86 °C in 2 min in a 20 °C room. Estimate the time for 71→69 °C.',steps:['For the first interval, mean excess temperature is 90−20=70 °C and cooling rate is 8/2=4 °C min⁻¹.','For the second, mean excess is 70−20=50 °C; rate scales to 4×50/70=2.86 °C min⁻¹.','Time for a 2 °C drop is 2/2.86=0.70 min.'],answer:'About 0.70 min = 42 s (small-excess, mean-temperature approximation).'}
  ]}
];

// Keep worked material beside its syllabus topic: NCERT Examples 10.3 and
// 10.4 demonstrate calorimetry and phase change, respectively.
const specificHeatTopic=concepts.find(c=>c.id==='sec-10-6');
const calorimetryExample=specificHeatTopic.examples.shift();
const fusionExample=specificHeatTopic.examples.shift();
concepts.find(c=>c.id==='sec-10-7').examples=[calorimetryExample];
concepts.find(c=>c.id==='sec-10-8').examples.unshift(fusionExample);

const exercises = [
  {n:1,c:'sec-10-2',q:'The triple points of neon and carbon dioxide are 24.57 K and 216.55 K respectively. Express these temperatures on the Celsius and Fahrenheit scales.',s:['Use tC=T−273.15 and tF=(9/5)tC+32.','Neon: −248.58 °C, then −415.44 °F.','CO₂: −56.60 °C, then −69.88 °F.'],a:'Ne: −248.58 °C (−415.44 °F); CO₂: −56.60 °C (−69.88 °F).'},
  {n:2,c:'sec-10-2',q:'Two absolute scales A and B have triple points of water defined to be 200 A and 350 B. What is the relation between T<sub>A</sub> and T<sub>B</sub>?',s:['Absolute scales have the same zero; their readings are proportional.','At water’s triple point, TA/TB=200/350=4/7.'],a:'TA=(4/7)TB (equivalently TB=(7/4)TA).'},
  {n:3,c:'sec-10-2',q:'The electrical resistance in ohms of a certain thermometer varies with temperature according to R=R₀[1+α(T−T₀)]. The resistance is 101.6 Ω at the triple-point of water 273.16 K, and 165.5 Ω at the normal melting point of lead (600.5 K). What is the temperature when the resistance is 123.4 Ω?',s:['Linear interpolation in resistance gives T=273.16+(123.4−101.6)(600.5−273.16)/(165.5−101.6).','Substitute the resistance differences and evaluate.'],a:'T≈384.8 K.'},
  {n:4,c:'sec-10-2',q:'Answer the following: (a) The triple-point of water is a standard fixed point in modern thermometry. Why? What is wrong in taking the melting point of ice and the boiling point of water as standard fixed points (as was originally done in the Celsius scale)? (b) There were two fixed points in the original Celsius scale as mentioned above which were assigned the number 0 °C and 100 °C respectively. On the absolute scale, one of the fixed points is the triple-point of water, which on the Kelvin absolute scale is assigned the number 273.16 K. What is the other fixed point on this (Kelvin) scale? (c) The absolute temperature (Kelvin scale) T is related to the temperature t<sub>C</sub> on the Celsius scale by t<sub>C</sub>=T−273.15. Why do we have 273.15 in this relation, and not 273.16? (d) What is the temperature of the triple-point of water on an absolute scale whose unit interval size is equal to that of the Fahrenheit scale?',s:['(a) The triple point is a unique state; ice/steam points shift with pressure and impurities.','(b) The other defining reference is absolute zero, 0 K.','(c) 273.16 K is the triple point, 0.01 K above water’s melting point at 1 atm, which defines 0 °C; hence the Celsius offset is 273.15.','(d) A Fahrenheit-sized interval is 5/9 K, so one such absolute degree is 9/5 K.'],a:'(d) 273.16×9/5=491.688 degrees on that absolute scale.'},
  {n:5,c:'sec-10-3',q:'Two ideal gas thermometers A and B use oxygen and hydrogen respectively. The following observations are made: at the triple-point of water the pressures are 1.250×10⁵ Pa (A) and 0.200×10⁵ Pa (B); at the normal melting point of sulphur they are 1.797×10⁵ Pa (A) and 0.287×10⁵ Pa (B). (a) What is the absolute temperature of normal melting point of sulphur as read by thermometers A and B? (b) What do you think is the reason behind the slight difference in answers (the thermometers are not faulty). What further procedure is needed in the experiment to reduce the discrepancy?',s:['At fixed volume, T/Ttp=P/Ptp. For A, T=273.16×1.797/1.250.','For B, T=273.16×0.287/0.200.','Real gases deviate slightly at finite pressure; repeat at lower gas densities and extrapolate to zero pressure.'],a:'A: about 392.6 K; B: about 392.4 K. Zero-pressure extrapolation reduces the difference.'},
  {n:6,c:'sec-10-4',q:'A steel tape 1 m long is correctly calibrated for a temperature of 27.0 °C. The length of a steel rod measured by this tape is found to be 63.0 cm on a hot day when the temperature is 45.0 °C. What is the actual length of the steel rod on that day? What is the length of the same steel rod on a day when the temperature is 27.0 °C? Coefficient of linear expansion of steel = 1.20×10⁻⁵ K⁻¹.',s:['At 45 °C one tape metre is 1[1+α(18)]=1.000216 m, so the indicated 0.630 m represents 0.630136 m.','The tape and rod are the same material, so both expand by the same factor from 27 to 45 °C.','Divide the hot-day physical length by 1.000216 to recover its 27 °C length.'],a:'Hot-day length ≈0.63014 m; length at 27 °C =0.63000 m.'},
  {n:7,c:'sec-10-4',q:'A large steel wheel is to be fitted on to a shaft of the same material. At 27 °C, the outer diameter of the shaft is 8.70 cm and the diameter of the central hole in the wheel is 8.69 cm. The shaft is cooled using “dry ice”. At what temperature of the shaft does the wheel slip on the shaft? Assume coefficient of linear expansion of steel to be constant over the required temperature range: αsteel=1.20×10⁻⁵ K⁻¹.',s:['The shaft must contract to 8.69 cm: 8.69=8.70[1+α(T−27)].','Solve T=27+(8.69/8.70−1)/(1.20×10⁻⁵).'],a:'T≈−68.8 °C; cooling to dry-ice temperature is sufficient.'},
  {n:8,c:'sec-10-4',q:'A hole is drilled in a copper sheet. The diameter of the hole is 4.24 cm at 27.0 °C. What is the change in the diameter of the hole when the sheet is heated to 227 °C? Coefficient of linear expansion of copper = 1.70×10⁻⁵ K⁻¹.',s:['A hole expands as if it were filled with the same material: Δd=αdΔT.','Δd=1.70×10⁻⁵×4.24 cm×200.'],a:'Δd=0.0144 cm=0.144 mm (increase).'},
  {n:9,c:'sec-10-5',q:'A brass wire 1.8 m long at 27 °C is held taut with little tension between two rigid supports. If the wire is cooled to a temperature of −39 °C, what is the tension developed in the wire, if its diameter is 2.0 mm? Coefficient of linear expansion of brass =2.0×10⁻⁵ K⁻¹; Young’s modulus of brass =0.91×10¹¹ Pa.',s:['Cooling by 66 K would cause tensile strain α|ΔT| if free; the supports prevent it.','σ=Yα|ΔT|=0.91×10¹¹×2×10⁻⁵×66=1.201×10⁸ Pa.','A=π(1.0×10⁻³)² m²; tension F=σA.'],a:'F≈3.77×10² N (about 377 N).'},
  {n:10,c:'sec-10-5',q:'A brass rod of length 50 cm and diameter 3.0 mm is joined to a steel rod of the same length and diameter. What is the change in length of the combined rod at 250 °C, if the original lengths are at 40.0 °C? Is there a “thermal stress” developed at the junction? The ends of the rod are free to expand (co-efficient of linear expansion of brass =2.0×10⁻⁵ K⁻¹, steel =1.2×10⁻⁵ K⁻¹).',s:['Each rod warms by 210 K. ΔLtotal=0.50(2.0×10⁻⁵)(210)+0.50(1.2×10⁻⁵)(210).','Because the outer ends are free, each section expands by its own free amount; no constraint requires elastic strain.'],a:'Total increase =3.36×10⁻³ m=3.36 mm; no thermal stress is required at the junction.'},
  {n:11,c:'sec-10-5',q:'The coefficient of volume expansion of glycerine is 49×10⁻⁵ K⁻¹. What is the fractional change in its density for a 30 °C rise in temperature?',s:['At fixed mass, ρ= m/V. With V₂/V₁=1+γΔT, Δρ/ρ=1/(1+γΔT)−1.','Here γΔT=49×10⁻⁵×30=0.0147.'],a:'Δρ/ρ≈−0.0145 (about a 1.45% decrease; the small-change estimate is −1.47%).'},
  {n:12,c:'sec-10-6',q:'A 10 kW drilling machine is used to drill a bore in a small aluminium block of mass 8.0 kg. How much is the rise in temperature of the block in 2.5 minutes, assuming 50% of power is used up in heating the machine itself or lost to the surroundings. Specific heat of aluminium =0.91 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹.',s:['Useful heat=0.50×10,000 W×150 s=750,000 J.','The block heat capacity is 8000 g×0.91 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹=7280 J K⁻¹.'],a:'ΔT≈103 K.'},
  {n:13,c:'sec-10-8',q:'A copper block of mass 2.5 kg is heated in a furnace to a temperature of 500 °C and then placed on a large ice block. What is the maximum amount of ice that can melt? (Specific heat of copper =0.39 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹; heat of fusion of water =335 J g⁻¹.)',s:['The block cools to 0 °C and releases Q=2.5×390×500=487,500 J.','That heat melts m=Q/Lf=487,500/335,000 kg.'],a:'About 1.46 kg of ice.'},
  {n:14,c:'sec-10-7',q:'In an experiment on the specific heat of a metal, a 0.20 kg block of the metal at 150 °C is dropped in a copper calorimeter (of water equivalent 0.025 kg) containing 150 cm³ of water at 27 °C. The final temperature is 40 °C. Compute the specific heat of the metal. If heat losses to the surroundings are not negligible, is your answer greater or smaller than the actual value for specific heat of the metal?',s:['Water plus calorimeter water-equivalent mass is 0.150+0.025=0.175 kg. Heat gained=0.175×4186×(40−27)=9.52×10³ J.','Metal loses 0.20s(150−40)=22s joules. Equate to heat gained.','If heat escaped outside, the metal gave more heat than the water/calorimeter received, so ignoring that loss underestimates s.'],a:'s≈433 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹; with unaccounted heat loss the calculated value is smaller than the actual value.'},
  {n:15,c:'sec-10-6',q:'Given below are observations on molar specific heats at room temperature of some common gases: H₂ 4.87, N₂ 4.97, O₂ 5.02, NO 4.99, CO 5.01, Cl₂ 6.17 cal mol⁻¹ K⁻¹. The measured molar specific heats of these gases are markedly different from those for monatomic gases. Typically, molar specific heat of a monatomic gas is 2.92 cal mol⁻¹ K⁻¹. Explain this difference. What can you infer from the somewhat larger (than the rest) value for chlorine?',s:['Diatomic molecules store thermal energy in rotational as well as translational degrees of freedom; additional vibrational modes can contribute.','The larger Cl₂ value indicates that more internal vibrational energy is accessible at room temperature than for the other listed gases.'],a:'Molecular internal degrees of freedom raise molar heat capacity above the monatomic value; Cl₂’s higher value signals greater vibrational contribution.'},
  {n:16,c:'sec-10-6',q:'A child running a temperature of 101 °F is given an antipyrin (i.e. a medicine that lowers fever) which causes an increase in the rate of evaporation of sweat from his body. If the fever is brought down to 98 °F in 20 minutes, what is the average rate of extra evaporation caused by the drug. Assume the evaporation mechanism to be the only way by which heat is lost. The mass of the child is 30 kg. The specific heat of human body is approximately the same as that of water, and latent heat of evaporation of water at that temperature is about 580 cal g⁻¹.',s:['A 3 °F fall is ΔT=(5/9)×3=1.667 K. Heat removed≈30×4186×1.667=2.09×10⁵ J.','Latent heat 580 cal g⁻¹≈2.43×10⁶ J kg⁻¹; evaporated mass≈0.086 kg.','Divide by 20 min.'],a:'About 4.3 g min⁻¹ of extra evaporation.'},
  {n:17,c:'sec-10-9',q:'A “thermacole” icebox is a cheap and an efficient method for storing small quantities of cooked food in summer in particular. A cubical icebox of side 30 cm has a thickness of 5.0 cm. If 4.0 kg of ice is put in the box, estimate the amount of ice remaining after 6 h. The outside temperature is 45 °C, and co-efficient of thermal conductivity of thermacole is 0.01 J s⁻¹ m⁻¹ K⁻¹. [Heat of fusion of water =335×10³ J kg⁻¹]',s:['Approximate area of six outside faces A=6(0.30)²=0.54 m²; inside stays near 0 °C.','Conducted heat Q=K A ΔT t/L=0.01×0.54×45×21600/0.05≈1.05×10⁵ J.','Melted ice≈Q/Lf≈0.313 kg; subtract from 4.0 kg.'],a:'About 3.69 kg remains (rough estimate; edge losses and temperature changes are neglected).'},
  {n:18,c:'sec-10-9',q:'A brass boiler has a base area of 0.15 m² and thickness 1.0 cm. It boils water at the rate of 6.0 kg/min when placed on a gas stove. Estimate the temperature of the part of the flame in contact with the boiler. Thermal conductivity of brass =109 J s⁻¹ m⁻¹ K⁻¹; heat of vaporisation of water =2256×10³ J kg⁻¹.',s:['Heat current into boiling water=ṁLv=(6/60)×2.256×10⁶=225,600 W.','Conduction through the base gives H=KA(Tflame−100 °C)/L.','Solve ΔT=HL/(KA)=225600×0.01/(109×0.15).'],a:'Flame-contact temperature≈238 °C.'},
  {n:19,c:'sec-10-9-radiation',q:'Explain why: (a) a body with large reflectivity is a poor emitter; (b) a brass tumbler feels much colder than a wooden tray on a chilly day; (c) an optical pyrometer (for measuring high temperatures) calibrated for an ideal black body radiation gives too low a value for the temperature of a red hot iron piece in the open, but gives a correct value for the temperature when the same piece is in the furnace; (d) the earth without its atmosphere would be inhospitably cold; (e) heating systems based on circulation of steam are more efficient in warming a building than those based on circulation of hot water.',s:['(a) At thermal equilibrium, strong reflection means weak absorption and hence weak emission.','(b) Brass conducts heat away from skin much faster than wood, so the skin cools faster.','(c) Open iron has emissivity below 1, so a blackbody-calibrated pyrometer under-reads; inside a hot furnace reflected radiation adds and the cavity approaches blackbody conditions.','(d) The atmosphere absorbs and re-emits outgoing infrared radiation, warming the surface.','(e) Steam releases latent heat when it condenses, in addition to sensible heat.'],a:'Each effect follows from emissivity/absorptivity, conductivity, cavity radiation, atmospheric infrared trapping or latent heat.'},
  {n:20,c:'sec-10-10',q:'A body cools from 80 °C to 50 °C in 5 minutes. Calculate the time it takes to cool from 60 °C to 30 °C. The temperature of the surroundings is 20 °C.',s:['For the first interval the mean body temperature is 65 °C, so mean excess is 45 °C; rate≈30/5=6 °C min⁻¹.','For the second interval mean body temperature is 45 °C, so mean excess is 25 °C. Newton’s small-interval approximation gives rate≈6×25/45=3.33 °C min⁻¹.','Time≈30/3.33.'],a:'About 9 min by the textbook mean-excess approximation. Exact exponential integration gives 10 min; Newton’s law is approximate over these finite intervals.'}
];

const quiz = [
 ['sec-10-1','Which statement defines heat?',['Energy stored in a hot body','Energy transferred due to a temperature difference','A measure of mass','The same quantity as temperature'],1,'Heat is energy in transit driven by a temperature difference.','Heat is transfer; internal energy is stored in the system.'],
 ['sec-10-1','At thermal equilibrium between two bodies, the net heat flow is:',['From lighter to heavier','Zero','Always into the solid','Proportional to their masses'],1,'Equal temperatures remove the temperature difference that drives net heat transfer.','Thermal equilibrium means equal temperature.'],
 ['sec-10-1','The SI units of heat and temperature are respectively:',['calorie and °C','joule and kelvin','watt and kelvin','joule and °C'],1,'Heat is measured in joules and thermodynamic temperature in kelvin.','Use J for energy and K for temperature.'],
 ['sec-10-2','A temperature of 20 °C equals:',['36 °F','52 °F','68 °F','293 °F'],2,'tF=(9/5)20+32=68 °F.','Convert Celsius by multiplying by 9/5, then add 32.'],
 ['sec-10-2','Which thermometric property is commonly used in a liquid-in-glass thermometer?',['Electrical charge','Liquid volume','Mass','Surface tension only'],1,'The liquid volume changes with temperature and moves the column.','Thermometers infer temperature from a changing physical property.'],
 ['sec-10-2','Why is the triple point useful as a standard fixed point?',['It depends on vessel shape','It is a unique reproducible state with coexisting phases','It occurs at every pressure','It is exactly 0 °C'],1,'The specified three-phase equilibrium state is reproducible and unique.','Fixed points must be reproducible.'],
 ['sec-10-3','At constant volume, an ideal gas has:',['P/T constant','PT constant','P/V constant','P independent of T'],0,'From PV=μRT at fixed V, P/T=μR/V is constant.','At fixed volume, gas pressure is proportional to kelvin temperature.'],
 ['sec-10-3','The ideal-gas equation is:',['PV=μRT','P=μVT','PV=RT/μ','PT=μRV'],0,'For μ moles, PV=μRT.','Temperature in this equation must be absolute.'],
 ['sec-10-3','The Kelvin and Celsius scales have:',['Different degree intervals','Equal-sized intervals and different zeroes','Same zero','A ratio of 273.15 between intervals'],1,'Their intervals are equal; T=tC+273.15.','Kelvin shifts the origin, not the interval size.'],
 ['sec-10-4','A 2 m steel rod with α=1.2×10⁻⁵ K⁻¹ warms by 10 K. Its extension is:',['0.024 mm','0.24 mm','2.4 mm','24 mm'],1,'ΔL=αLΔT=2.4×10⁻⁴ m=0.24 mm.','Check units after using ΔL=αLΔT.'],
 ['sec-10-4','For a small temperature rise, area expansivity β of an isotropic solid is approximately:',['α/3','α','2α','3α'],2,'Two in-plane dimensions each expand by αΔT, giving β≈2α.','Area expansion has two linear directions.'],
 ['sec-10-4','A circular hole in a heated sheet:',['Shrinks','Stays the same','Expands as if the hole material were present','Expands only if the sheet is liquid'],2,'Every linear dimension of the sheet, including the boundary of the hole, scales by the same factor.','A hole expands when the surrounding solid expands.'],
 ['sec-10-5','At what temperature is water densest near ordinary pressure?',['0 °C','4 °C','25 °C','100 °C'],1,'Water contracts on warming from 0 to 4 °C and has maximum density at 4 °C.','Water’s expansion is anomalous below 4 °C.'],
 ['sec-10-5','For an ideal gas at constant pressure, the volume expansivity is:',['T','1/T','T²','Independent of T'],1,'V∝T, so (1/V)(dV/dT)=1/T.','The gas expansivity depends on absolute temperature.'],
 ['sec-10-5','The magnitude of stress in a fully restrained rod heated by ΔT is:',['YαΔT','Y/(αΔT)','αΔT/Y','YΔT/α'],0,'The imposed elastic strain cancels free thermal strain, so stress magnitude is YαΔT.','Constraint converts expansion strain into stress.'],
 ['sec-10-6','Specific heat capacity s is:',['Heat per unit mass per unit temperature change','Heat capacity times mass','Heat per unit volume only','Energy per degree for any whole body'],0,'s=ΔQ/(mΔT).','Distinguish specific heat from whole-body heat capacity.'],
 ['sec-10-6','Why do we specify Cp or Cv for a gas?',['Gas mass changes','Heat capacity depends on whether pressure or volume is held fixed','Kelvin changes size','A gas has no internal energy'],1,'The work and resulting temperature rise depend on the constraint.','Gas molar heat capacity needs a process condition.'],
 ['sec-10-6','For heating with no phase change, the required heat is:',['Q=mL','Q=msΔT','Q=s/(mΔT)','Q=PV'],1,'The definition of specific heat gives Q=msΔT.','Use sensible heat within a single phase.'],
 ['sec-10-7','In an ideal isolated calorimetry experiment:',['Heat gained exceeds heat lost','Sum of signed heat transfers is zero','Final temperature is always 0 °C','Only water absorbs heat'],1,'Energy conservation gives ΣQ=0 when the system is isolated.','Include the calorimeter in the energy balance.'],
 ['sec-10-7','A calorimeter’s water equivalent represents:',['Its mass only','Its heat capacity expressed as an equivalent mass of water','Its volume in litres','Its latent heat'],1,'It is the mass of water with the same heat capacity.','Add the equivalent water mass to the sample water mass.'],
 ['sec-10-7','If the final mixture temperature is below the melting point of ice, ice originally at that melting point will:',['Remain partly unmelted','All become steam','Warm without changing phase','Have negative latent heat'],0,'There is no energy available to melt all the ice and reach a lower final temperature.','Check phase assumptions against the energy balance.'],
 ['sec-10-8','During melting at fixed pressure, the temperature of a pure substance:',['Rises continuously','Remains constant while phases coexist','Falls to absolute zero','Depends on its mass'],1,'Added energy supplies latent heat while solid and liquid coexist.','A phase plateau stores energy without raising temperature.'],
 ['sec-10-8','Latent heat of fusion per unit mass has SI unit:',['J kg⁻¹','J K⁻¹','W m⁻¹','kg J⁻¹'],0,'Lf=Q/m.','Latent heat is energy per mass.'],
 ['sec-10-8','Evaporation differs from boiling because evaporation:',['Occurs only at 100 °C','Occurs at the surface below boiling point','Requires the whole liquid to reach boiling','Always heats the liquid'],1,'Surface molecules can escape below the boiling temperature, cooling the liquid.','Evaporation can happen at many temperatures.'],
 ['sec-10-8-pressure','At the triple point, which phases coexist?',['Solid and liquid only','Liquid and vapour only','Solid, liquid and vapour','Two gases'],2,'All three phase-boundary curves meet at the triple point.','Triple point means three phases in equilibrium.'],
 ['sec-10-8-pressure','Why does water’s fusion curve have negative slope?',['Ice is denser than water','Liquid water is denser than ice','Water has no latent heat','Pressure is zero'],1,'Pressure favours the denser liquid phase, lowering the melting temperature.','Ice expands on melting because it is less dense.'],
 ['sec-10-8-pressure','At ordinary atmospheric pressure, dry ice (solid CO₂) usually:',['Melts into liquid','Sublimes directly to gas','Freezes water','Remains above its triple point as liquid'],1,'The 1 atm path does not cross the liquid region of the CO₂ phase diagram.','Sublimation is solid-to-vapour transition.'],
 ['sec-10-9','Which heat-transfer mode can occur through a vacuum?',['Conduction','Convection','Radiation','None'],2,'Electromagnetic radiation needs no material medium.','Sunlight warms Earth through space by radiation.'],
 ['sec-10-9','For an insulated bar at steady state, the heat current is proportional to:',['L/(KAΔT)','KAΔT/L','KL/(AΔT)','A/(KLΔT)'],1,'Fourier’s steady one-dimensional relation is H=KAΔT/L.','Longer bars resist conduction; larger area/conductivity increase it.'],
 ['sec-10-9','Natural convection is mainly driven by:',['Electrical charge','Buoyancy from density differences','Molecular diffusion alone','Surface tension'],1,'Heating changes fluid density; buoyant warm fluid rises and cooler fluid sinks.','Convection transports heat with moving fluid.'],
 ['sec-10-9-radiation','Wien’s displacement law predicts that when temperature rises, λmax:',['Increases','Decreases','Stays fixed','Becomes zero at 100 °C'],1,'λmaxT is constant, so a higher T means a shorter peak wavelength.','Hotter stars look bluer because the peak shifts shorter.'],
 ['sec-10-9-radiation','A perfect blackbody has emissivity:',['0','0.4','1','Greater than 1'],2,'A blackbody is the ideal emitter and absorber, e=1.','Emissivity is a fraction from 0 to 1.'],
 ['sec-10-9-radiation','The net radiation from a body in surroundings at Ts is proportional to:',['T−Ts only at all temperatures','T⁴−Ts⁴','T²+Ts²','1/Ts'],1,'Net power is eσA(T⁴−Ts⁴).','Temperatures in radiation laws are in kelvin.'],
 ['sec-10-10','Newton’s law of cooling is a good approximation when:',['Body and surroundings differ by a small temperature','The body is in vacuum only','The body is boiling','Temperature is measured in Celsius only'],0,'The linear rate law assumes a small temperature excess.','Do not extend the linear cooling law to arbitrary large differences.'],
 ['sec-10-10','For Newton cooling, ln(T−Ts) plotted against time is:',['A straight line with negative slope','A circle','A horizontal line','A parabola'],0,'T−Ts=(T₀−Ts)e⁻ᴷᵗ; taking logs gives slope −K.','Exponential excess temperature becomes linear on a log plot.'],
 ['sec-10-10','A body’s temperature approaches ambient under Newton cooling:',['Linearly and reaches it in finite time','Exponentially, approaching it asymptotically','Instantly','At a constant rate'],1,'The excess decays exponentially and tends to zero asymptotically.','The cooling rate slows as the excess falls.']
];

