<section class="ncert-section" id="sec-10-ponder"><div class="section-header"><span class="section-num-badge">CHECK</span><h2>Points to keep in mind</h2></div><div class="concept-card"><ul><li>Kelvin’s zero and its unit interval are defined by the scale; 273.16 K is water’s triple point, while 0 °C at 1 atm is 273.15 K.</li><li>Coexisting phases have the same equilibrium temperature and pressure, but different molar volumes and densities.</li><li>Heat transfer requires a temperature difference; energy transfer without that cause is not heat.</li><li>A hot bar under running water loses heat to the water by conduction at the interface; convection is the subsequent bulk motion within the fluid.</li></ul></div></section>
